The problem here is that you're using `std::vector` instead of `std::array`. The former doesn't have a constructor that takes an initializer list, so you can't do `std::vector{...}`.

Change your code to:

```
std::array<float, 5> row = {0.160625f, 0.967468297f, 3.520480583f, 0.862454481f, -0.341933766f};
inputs.push_back(torch::tensor(row));
```

I'm not sure what you mean by "Curriculum Dropout", but I assume it's something like this:

```
class MyModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, p=0.5):
        super().__init__()
        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(p)

    def forward(self, x):
        return self.dropout(x)
```

Then you can adjust `p` during training.

If you need to change the dropout probability dynamically (i.e., per batch), then you can override [`forward`](http://pytorch.org/docs/master/nn.html#torch.nn.Module.forward) method:

```
class MyModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, p=0.5):
        super().__init__()
        self.dropout = nn.Dropout()

    def forward(self, x, p=0.5):
        return self.dropout(x, p=p)
```

The problem is that you're not setting the `position` of your element to `relative`. This means that when you try to animate the `left` property, nothing happens.

Try adding:

```
#asd {
  position: relative;
}
```
